1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,892
Welcome to practicals seven and eight:

2
00:00:02,892 --> 00:00:06,013
univariate E D A on a large real dataset.

3
00:00:06,013 --> 00:00:16,896
We use the Superstore orders, eight thousand three hundred ninety nine rows and twenty one columns, with full code, results and interpretation.

4
00:00:17,696 --> 00:00:22,059
The source is eight thousand real retail order records.

5
00:00:22,059 --> 00:00:24,359
We have six numeric features:

6
00:00:24,359 --> 00:00:35,623
sales, profit, discount, order quantity, unit price and shipping cost; and categorical features like category, ship mode and customer segment.

7
00:00:35,623 --> 00:00:43,952
The goal is to describe every numeric feature fully, compare distributions, and write down what we learn.

8
00:00:44,752 --> 00:00:45,471
Step one:

9
00:00:45,471 --> 00:00:58,969
read the C S V into a data frame, check the shape for a fast sanity check on size, and print the data types to see which columns pandas treats as numeric or categorical.

10
00:00:58,969 --> 00:01:05,039
We get eight thousand three hundred ninety nine rows and twenty one columns.

11
00:01:05,039 --> 00:01:09,352
Always run this first, before any statistics or plots.

12
00:01:10,152 --> 00:01:10,905
Step two:

13
00:01:10,905 --> 00:01:18,016
isna flags every cell as true or false, and sum adds those up per column in one line.

14
00:01:18,016 --> 00:01:26,298
Only product base margin shows a real gap, sixty three missing rows, zero point seven five percent.

15
00:01:26,298 --> 00:01:30,816
All the columns we use today have zero missing values.

16
00:01:31,616 --> 00:01:32,586
Step three:

17
00:01:32,586 --> 00:01:36,201
full summary statistics in one line each.

18
00:01:36,201 --> 00:01:45,106
Mean, median, standard deviation, skew and kurtosis, each applied to all six numeric columns at once.

19
00:01:45,106 --> 00:01:52,424
No loops and no if statements; pandas broadcasts the statistic across every column.

20
00:01:53,224 --> 00:02:04,122
Sales, profit and unit price are extremely right-skewed, with skew of five point three nine, three point six five and fourteen point one three.

21
00:02:04,122 --> 00:02:07,476
Order quantity is nearly flat and symmetric.

22
00:02:07,476 --> 00:02:09,686
Discount is close to uniform.

23
00:02:09,686 --> 00:02:21,880
And profit's median is negative one fifty despite a positive mean of one hundred eighty one dollars, a strong early hint that many individual orders lose money.

24
00:02:22,680 --> 00:02:23,516
Step four:

25
00:02:23,516 --> 00:02:25,020
robust statistics.

26
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:30,534
The I Q R is Q three minus Q one for all six columns in two lines.

27
00:02:30,534 --> 00:02:40,561
The coefficient of variation, standard deviation over mean, lets us compare spread across features with different units.

28
00:02:40,561 --> 00:02:49,752
The outlier count uses the one point five I Q R rule applied to every column with a single boolean comparison.

29
00:02:50,552 --> 00:03:01,836
Profit flags the most outliers, one thousand seven hundred four orders, twenty percent, both unusually large profits and unusually large losses.

30
00:03:01,836 --> 00:03:07,243
Sales flags twelve percent, a small number of very large bulk orders.

31
00:03:07,243 --> 00:03:15,392
Order quantity and discount flag essentially zero, because they are naturally bounded and evenly spread.

32
00:03:16,192 --> 00:03:19,021
Step five is a real business finding.

33
00:03:19,021 --> 00:03:25,292
Checking what fraction of a column is negative is a simple but powerful one-liner.

34
00:03:25,292 --> 00:03:31,792
Combined with the median from step three, it tells us something the mean alone hides.

35
00:03:32,592 --> 00:03:41,137
Fifty point eight percent of all orders, just over half, lose money, and the median order profit is minus one fifty.

36
00:03:41,137 --> 00:03:42,611
The mean hides this:

37
00:03:42,611 --> 00:03:52,703
the average profit is a positive one hundred eighty one dollars purely because a smaller number of very profitable orders pull it upward.

38
00:03:52,703 --> 00:03:59,849
Whenever mean and median diverge this much, the mean alone is misleading about the typical order.

39
00:03:59,849 --> 00:04:09,720
This raises a business question about which categories or discount levels drive the losses, which practical nine and ten will address.

40
00:04:10,520 --> 00:04:11,240
Step six:

41
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:14,600
comparing distributions across a category.

42
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:20,360
We loop over each product category and overlay its profit density curve.

43
00:04:20,360 --> 00:04:29,960
This is the one place a simple loop is clearer than a one-liner, because we are drawing several curves on the same axes.

44
00:04:30,760 --> 00:04:37,846
All three categories centre close to zero, with profitable and loss-making orders in each.

45
00:04:37,846 --> 00:04:43,672
Technology has a visibly wider spread, with bigger wins and bigger losses.

46
00:04:43,672 --> 00:04:54,064
Technology averages four hundred twenty nine dollars per order versus sixty eight for furniture, a real, meaningful category effect.

47
00:04:54,864 --> 00:04:55,743
Step seven:

48
00:04:55,743 --> 00:05:03,090
value counts is the categorical equivalent of describe, one line for a full frequency table.

49
00:05:03,090 --> 00:05:14,112
Run it once per categorical column, and always check these before any grouping or cross-tab analysis, to know each category's sample size.

50
00:05:14,912 --> 00:05:22,529
Office supplies dominates with fifty five percent of orders; furniture is smallest at twenty percent.

51
00:05:22,529 --> 00:05:29,694
Regular air is the overwhelming shipping choice at seventy five percent; express air is rarest.

52
00:05:29,694 --> 00:05:36,632
Corporate is the largest segment at thirty seven percent, with the other three fairly close.

53
00:05:37,432 --> 00:05:46,454
A pie chart divides a whole into proportional slices, ideal here since product category has exactly three groups.

54
00:05:46,454 --> 00:05:50,845
Office supplies makes up well over half of every order.

55
00:05:50,845 --> 00:05:51,644
A caution:

56
00:05:51,644 --> 00:06:01,384
pie charts get hard to read past four or five slices, so a region variable with eight categories is better as a bar chart.

57
00:06:02,184 --> 00:06:02,907
To recap:

58
00:06:02,907 --> 00:06:09,497
six one-line pandas commands fully describe six numeric features, no loops needed.

59
00:06:09,497 --> 00:06:18,417
Sales, profit and unit price are strongly right-skewed, while order quantity and discount are close to uniform.

60
00:06:18,417 --> 00:06:27,338
Just over half of all orders, fifty point eight percent, lose money, a finding the mean alone completely hides.

61
00:06:27,338 --> 00:06:37,705
And comparing distributions across categories is how the first real hypotheses form, which we continue in practical nine and ten.

62
00:06:37,705 --> 00:06:39,312
Thanks for watching.

